{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791621361,"nonce":"7kQIh4wdmpTz77EHVQNaGMgD2WU=","message":"Cause found, and it is not the guard. Measured on 3d5ab48 with the committed reproducer's own bytecode (control and guarded bench.hl from the first comment's artifacts).\n\n1. The guard moves where the time is counted, not how much there is. `RigLoad.skeleton` is a lazy getter: `get_skeleton() { ensureRig(); ... }`, and `ensureRig()` runs `GltfImporter.buildRig`, the whole rig build. In the guarded variant the first read of `model.skeleton` is the added line, inside the loadMs interval. In the control the first read is in `DeformationCharacter.new`, after `loaded` is taken. The promotion traces agree: the last tier install is at t=24077 ms in the control and 22561 ms in the guarded run, with a gap of 15.2 s and 12.8 s between installs in each.\n\n2. That work runs in the interpreter. ASH_PROFILE=all on the control: 29.2 s wall (machine loaded), 82.0% of samples in the interpreter, 3.1% in Cranelift code, 0.7% in LLVM code; 84.6% of samples have findex 869 on the interpreter's stack. Fun_869 is a 2258-op method called once, with 29 backward jumps; Cranelift installed it at t=0.8 s, so the running activation never gets there.\n\n3. Same program, same bytecode, by engine: `--mode jit` 4 s wall (loadMs 433, cold frame 103); hybrid 25-29 s; `--mode interp` 86 s. So the hybrid ladder leaves 6x on the table here.\n\n4. Why OSR does not rescue it. ASH_OSR_LOG=1 reports 23 hot loop headers in findex 869, every one refused: `[RefTaken, MultipleLatches(330), MultipleLatches(197), MultipleLatches(124), MultipleLatches(8), NoBackEdge]`. crates/ash/src/osr.rs refuses a header when it is reachable from any block that takes a Ref, and the function begins with `Ref r32 = \u0026r28; Call2 fun(r28, r32)` patterns (50 Ref ops, each pointer dead right after its call), so every later header is \"reachable\". It also refuses the whole function, not just the loop, when any one loop has several latches (continue statements).\n\nDirection: make the Ref hazard depend on whether the pointer can still be live at the header (liveness, plus no escape other than as a call argument), and refuse per loop instead of per function. The fix belongs in osr::analyze; I am taking it on.\n\nRetitling: the guard is not the cause.","files":null}]}